\section{Setup: a JEPA near its collapsed solution}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Model.}
An input $x\in\R^n$ is split into a context view $x_c$ and a target view $x_t$; for masking, $x_c=M_cx$ and $x_t=M_tx$ with random binary masks $M_c,M_t$ drawn independently of $x$.
A context encoder $\Wc$ maps the context view to $z=\Wc x_c\in\R^d$, a target encoder $\Wt$ maps the target view to $\bar z=\Wt x_t$, and a predictor $\Pred$ predicts $\bar z$ from $z$.
Training minimises
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal L(\Wc,\Pred)=\tfrac12\,\E\big\|\Pred\Wc x_c-\sg(\Wt x_t)\big\|^2+\tfrac{\lambda_W}{2}\|\Wc\|_F^2+\tfrac{\lambda_P}{2}\|\Pred\|_F^2,
  \label{eq:loss}
\end{equation}
where $\sg$ is the stop-gradient and $\lambda_W,\lambda_P\ge0$ are the weight decays of the encoder and the predictor (in our experiments $\lambda_W=\lambda_P=\lambda$; we keep them apart as their roles differ).
The target encoder is not trained by gradient; after every optimiser step of size $\eta$ it follows the context encoder by an exponential moving average (EMA), $\Wt\leftarrow\tau\Wt+(1-\tau)\Wc$, with $\tau=0$ the plain stop-gradient copy of SimSiam and $\tau\to1$ a slowly moving target as in BYOL and I-JEPA. As in these methods, the target starts as a copy of the context encoder, $\Wt(0)=\Wc(0)$.
We analyse the gradient flow of the linear model, $\Wc\in\R^{d\times n}$, $\Pred\in\R^{d\times d}$, with time measured in units of the encoder learning rate, a predictor learning-rate ratio $\alpha$, and the EMA written as a relaxation at rate $\kappa=(1-\tau)/\eta$, which is its continuous-time limit for $1-\tau\ll1$ (for $\tau=0$ we set $\Wt=\Wc$ directly):
\begin{align}
  \dot\Wc&=\Pred^\top\Wt\,\Sigma_{tc}-\Pred^\top\Pred\,\Wc\,\Sigma_c-\lambda_W\Wc, \label{eq:flowW}\\
  \dot\Pred&=\alpha\big[(\Wt\,\Sigma_{tc}-\Pred\Wc\,\Sigma_c)\Wc^\top-\lambda_P\Pred\big], \label{eq:flowP}\\
  \dot{\Wt}&=\kappa\,(\Wc-\Wt). \label{eq:flowWt}
\end{align}
The data enter only through the context covariance $\Sigma_c=\E[x_cx_c^\top]$ and the target--context cross-covariance $\Sigma_{tc}=\E[x_tx_c^\top]$.
Because of the stop-gradient, \eqref{eq:flowW}--\eqref{eq:flowWt} are not the gradient flow of any single function, so we argue from the dynamics, not from a loss landscape.

\paragraph{Driving force and decay.}
Expanding the square in \eqref{eq:loss} gives, up to a term that carries no gradient,
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal L=\underbrace{-\,\E\langle \Pred z,\sg(\bar z)\rangle}_{\text{drive}}\;+\;\underbrace{\tfrac12\,\E\|\Pred z\|^2+\tfrac{\lambda_W}{2}\|\Wc\|_F^2+\tfrac{\lambda_P}{2}\|\Pred\|_F^2}_{\text{decay}} .
  \label{eq:split}
\end{equation}
The first term rewards predictions aligned with the target embedding; the second penalises the size of the prediction itself and the size of the weights; the split is defined for any encoder and predictor.
In the linear model it is the split of \eqref{eq:flowW} into the \emph{driving force} $\Pred^\top\Wt\Sigma_{tc}$, supplied only through the target and hence through the predictive signal $\Sigma_{tc}$ shared by the views, and the \emph{decay} $\Pred^\top\Pred\Wc\Sigma_c+\lambda_W\Wc$, supplied through the predictor's own output and by weight decay.

\paragraph{The collapsed solution.}
The collapsed state is $\Wc=\Wt=0$.
At it the predictor receives no data signal, since the first term of \eqref{eq:flowP} is quadratic in the embedding, and only decays, $\dot\Pred=-\alpha\lambda_P\Pred$.
For $\lambda_W,\lambda_P>0$ the stationary point $(\Wc,\Pred,\Wt)=(0,0,0)$ is therefore locally stable, with linearisation $\dot\Wc=-\lambda_W\Wc$, $\dot\Pred=-\alpha\lambda_P\Pred$, $\dot\Wt=\kappa(\Wc-\Wt)$, as in \citet{tian2021understanding}.
Collapse is consequently not decided by an instability of the collapsed point but by \emph{transient growth}: training starts with small embeddings and an order-one predictor, and a direction survives only if it grows to order one before the predictor has decayed.
The initial imbalance between the two is conserved up to weight decay: for $\alpha=1$ and $\lambda_W=\lambda_P=\lambda$, \eqref{eq:flowW}--\eqref{eq:flowP} give exactly (\cref{app:proofs})
\begin{equation}
  \Pred^\top\Pred-\Wc\Wc^\top=e^{-2\lambda t}\big(\Pred_0^\top\Pred_0-\Wc_0\Wc_0^\top\big)
  \label{eq:balance}
\end{equation}
for any $\Wt$, $\Sigma_c$ and $\Sigma_{tc}$, however large $\Wc$ has become.
This is Tian et al.'s conserved quantity (their Theorem~1) at $\alpha=1$; theirs holds for every $\alpha$ because they do not scale the predictor's decay by $\alpha$, as a per-group learning-rate multiplier does.
A standard initialisation is strongly unbalanced, $\Pred_0^\top\Pred_0\gg\Wc_0\Wc_0^\top$, the regime we treat: the drive is then first order in $\Wt$, whereas on the balanced manifold of Tian et al.'s weight-decay analysis it is quadratic.

\paragraph{Near-collapse regime and directions.}
While the embeddings are small, $\|\Wc\|^2\|\Sigma\|\ll\lambda_P\|\Pred\|$, the data term in \eqref{eq:flowP} is negligible, so to leading order $\Pred(t)=e^{-\alpha\lambda_P t}\Pred_0$ and \eqref{eq:flowW}, \eqref{eq:flowWt} are linear in $(\Wc,\Wt)$, until the first directions reach order one, when by our premise collapse is decided.
The operator acting on $\Wc$ in \eqref{eq:flowW} acts from both sides, and it splits into independent directions only under the following structure.
\begin{assumption}[Aligned statistics and predictor]
\label{ass:aligned}
(i) $\Sigma_c$ and $\Sigma_{tc}$ are symmetric and commute, with common orthonormal eigenvectors $u_k$, $\Sigma_cu_k=\sigma_ku_k$, $\Sigma_{tc}u_k=c_ku_k$;
(ii) $\Pred_0$ is symmetric with orthonormal eigenvectors $v_j$ and eigenvalues $p_j$.
\end{assumption}
Part (i) is a condition on the mask distribution, not exchangeability of context and target: with $\Sigma_c=\Sigma\circ G$, $\Sigma_{tc}=\Sigma\circ F$, $G_{ij}=\Pr(i\in c,\,j\in c)$ and $F_{ij}=\Pr(i\in t,\,j\in c)$, a stationary signal on a cyclic domain under translation- and reflection-invariant masks satisfies it, and I-JEPA-style block masks do so exactly; domain boundaries and oriented context--target geometry break it (\cref{app:structure}).
Part (ii) holds for $\Pred_0=p_0I$, not for a generic random predictor.
Under \cref{ass:aligned} the near-collapse dynamics decouple into scalar modes $w_{jk}=v_j^\top\Wc u_k$, each driven at rate $p_j(t)c_k$ through the target and decaying at rate $p_j(t)^2\sigma_k+\lambda_W$, with $p_j(t)=e^{-\alpha\lambda_Pt}p_j$ (\cref{app:structure}); the rank of $\Wc$ is generically the size of a maximum matching on the modes that reach order one.
For $\Pred_0=p_0I$ the gain does not depend on $j$, the rank is the number of surviving data directions $u_k$, and we call $u_k$ a \emph{direction}.
How several $v_j$ competing for the same $u_k$ resolve beyond the linear regime is not derived; we test the predicted rank also outside \cref{ass:aligned} (\cref{sec:experiments}).
